course: dictionary
lesson: 6851
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The right one depends on the context, which means the words and sentences around it. Outstanding is a good example: it can mean excellent, not yet paid, or easy to notice.
explain: In a school report, «outstanding work» is high praise. It means the work is extremely good, much better than most. The word «report» and the praise around it tell you which meaning is needed.
explain: On a bill, «outstanding balance» means money you still owe. If a bill is £50 and £30 is paid, £20 is outstanding. Here the clue is «balance» and «owe», so the meaning is not yet paid.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
fact: Outstanding means extremely good, much better than most.
fact: Outstanding can also mean not yet paid, done or sorted out, as in «outstanding balance».
fact: Outstanding can mean standing out and easy to notice, as in the riddle «outstanding in his field».
fact: «Let no debt remain outstanding» means no money should be left unpaid.
fact: One word can have several meanings, so always check the context.
exercise: The teacher wrote «outstanding work» on my report. What does outstanding mean here? | Not yet paid | Very wet | Easy to notice on the page | *Extremely good, much better than most
exercise: The bank says Dad has an «outstanding balance». What does outstanding mean? | Finished and paid in full | *Money still owed, not yet paid | Standing out in a crowd | Extremely good with lots of praise
exercise: In the riddle «outstanding in his field», what does outstanding mean? | *Standing out, easy to notice | Very old | Extremely good at farming | Not yet paid
test: Mum says «there are still outstanding jobs to do». What does outstanding mean? | *Still to be done | Extremely good | Glowing with light | Very old
test: In «Let no debt remain outstanding», what does outstanding mean? | *Not yet paid | Finished and celebrated | Very large in size | Easy to see
exam: Jayden reads «outstanding» in a school report and on a bill. What is the best way to know which meaning is right? | Ask someone else to read it for you | Always choose the first meaning in the dictionary | *Read the words around it and check the context | Guess from the length of the word
